<h2>What this meta tag generator includes</h2><p>The output covers a document title, description, robots directive, canonical URL, Open Graph tags and Twitter card metadata. These are common building blocks for search presentation, canonicalization and social sharing.</p><h2>How to use the generated tags</h2><ol><li>Enter metadata that accurately represents the page.</li><li>Use the canonical URL you want search engines to treat as the preferred version.</li><li>Select the robots directive deliberately.</li><li>Copy the generated markup into the page <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> or map the same values into your CMS SEO fields.</li></ol><h2>Why there is no meta keywords field</h2><p>This tool intentionally avoids legacy meta keywords. Modern page optimization should prioritize descriptive titles, useful content, crawlable structure, canonicals and relevant structured data instead of obsolete keyword stuffing fields.</p><h2>Social metadata is not the same as SEO metadata</h2><p>Open Graph and Twitter tags primarily control how links may appear when shared. They complement—but do not replace—the page title, meta description, canonical and visible page content.</p>
